      "sample_index": 4,
      "text": "To express the repeating decimal \\(0.5\\overline{10}\\) as a common fraction, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Let \\( x = 0.5\\overline{10} \\).\n2. To eliminate the repeating part, multiply \\( x \\) by 100 (since the repeating part \"10\" has two digits):\n   \\[\n   100x = 51.\\overline{10}\n   \\]\n3. Subtract the original \\( x = 0.5\\overline{10} \\) from this equation:\n   \\[\n   100x - x = 51.\\overline{10} - 0.5\\overline{10}\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   99x = 50.6\n   \\]\n4. Solve for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{50.6}{99}\n   \\]\n5. Convert \\( 50.6 \\) to a fraction:\n   \\[\n   50.6 = \\frac{506}{10}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{\\frac{506}{10}}{99} = \\frac{506}{10 \\cdot 99} = \\frac{506}{990}\n   \\]\n6. Simplify the fraction by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 506 and 990. The GCD of 506 and 990 is 2:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{506 \\div 2}{990 \\div 2} = \\frac{253}{495}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the common fraction representation of \\(0.5\\overline{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{253}{495}}\\).",
